    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "As a first step in understanding the mechanics of thrust faulting, we propose using the observed deformation of sedimentary horizons over a thrust fault to constrain the geometry of the underlying fault system. Boundary element models are used to forward model vertical displacements of marker horizons due to a buried thrust fault with varying geometrical parameters. Preliminary results reveal the most diagnostic features of changing parameters as: 1) asymmetry of the total displacement field for dip changes; 2) amplitude and half wavelength of the fold for changes in depth below the free surface; and 3) radius of curvature of the folded beds for changes in fault width or length. Qualitative characterization of the bedding surfaces of the Emigrant Gap anticline in Casper, Wyoming, is extracted from raster topography maps, digital orthoquad photographs, and field observations and data. The results of the boundary element models will be used to piece together a fault geometry that correlates well with this first order shape of the folded surfaces. The models indicate that the anticline is most likely underlain by several distinct, but interacting, fault segments. More analysis, including that of models containing variable slip distributions, is likely to be necessary to produce a satisfactory subsurface fault geometry.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
